# Styling Table Borders with CSS 🎨

By default, HTML tables have separated borders that look clunky. Use CSS `border-collapse` to create sleek borders:

```css_not
table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse; /* Merges adjacent borders into a single clean border */
}

th, td {
  border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
  padding: 12px 16px;
  text-align: left;
}

th {
  background-color: #f8fafc;
  color: #1e293b;
  font-weight: 600;
}
```

# Zebra Striping & Hover States 🦓

Zebra striping makes reading wide data rows substantially easier for users:

```css_not
/* Even rows get a subtle light background */
tbody tr:nth-child(even) {
  background-color: #f1f5f9;
}

/* Highlight row on mouse hover */
tbody tr:hover {
  background-color: #e2e8f0;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

```html_not
<!-- The CSS above automatically stripes alternating rows without needing custom classes -->
```

# Responsive Card Pattern (CSS Transform) 🃏

On very small screens, you can transform table rows into standalone stacked cards using CSS:

```css_not
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  table, thead, tbody, th, td, tr {
    display: block;
  }
  thead tr {
    position: absolute;
    top: -9999px; /* Hide headers visually */
  }
  tr {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 8px;
  }
  td {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 50%;
  }
}
```
